 {"id": "TJ-GAP-097", "title": "[P1] Resident bridge daemon can hang forever at 100% CPU in accept() after SIGINT/SIGTERM: the signal handler closes the listening socket, then serve()'s `except OSError: continue` turns the closed-fd error into a busy loop — the process never exits and the property test burns its 30s budget waiting on a daemon that is alive and spinning", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "created_at": "2026-10-22T22:38:00-05:00", "created_by": "terminal-jail-foreman", "depends_on": [], "blocks": [], "capability_tags": ["resident-bridge", "lifecycle", "signals", "resource-leak"], "complexity": 3, "attempts": 1, "blocked_reason": null, "description": "Observed live during this tick's post-merge full-suite gate on the merged main tree. pytest had been running 9 minutes with only 7s CPU and was blocked in anon_pipe_read; its child /home/kara/terminal-jail/.venv/bin/python plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor_resident.py --serve --sock .../test_sigint_removes_socket0/s.sock had been in state R for 7:47 with 7:45 CPU consumed. py-spy (run as root) showed the main thread inside `accept (socket.py:294)` called from `serve (plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor_resident.py:241)` — i.e. the accept loop, after the signal handler had already closed `server` via _cleanup(). The handler's SystemExit never terminated the process, and the closed fd makes accept() raise OSError on every iteration, so `except OSError: continue` spins forever. Killing the daemon unblocked pytest.", "reasoning": "Found while gating the INT-CI-014 merge (tick terminal-jail-foreman-2026-10-23-02-51-59). Evidence captured on the live process: /proc/<pid>/status 'State: R (running)', Threads 1, SigCgt 0x4002 (SIGINT+SIGTERM caught), 7:45 CPU in 7:47 elapsed; py-spy stack as above. Isolation control: the same 16-trial matrix (idle/mid-stream x SIGTERM/SIGINT, 4 trials each) run standalone with /tmp/tj_signal_spin_probe.py on an idle-ish box exits cleanly 16/16 (rc=143/130, socket removed every